Power BI, like many other modeling tools, lets us use images as data points within our models. Images are data that are stored in different formats than tabular data that we normally work with. We can connect to image data in online repositories via their URL links and store locally in folders. Here's what these images look like when we store them in an online repository like GitHub. You'll want to use smaller images if you can because Power BI has limitations on rendering images within visuals. You can resize them quickly in tools like Microsoft Paint if needed. To connect to these GitHub images, let's put the raw image URL links into a data table containing their names. We can then connect the name field to other tables in the Power BI model. When we connect to images from GitHub, we want to make sure to view the raw files denoted at the start of the URL. Next, let's connect to a locally stored image. On a high level, this process first involves importing the image data as a binary object into Power Query. We'll then convert it into a text bitmap with the help of a few formulas. Lastly, we'll load the bitmap into Power BI and convert it back into the image data by setting it as an image URL. We can use any connector because Power Query doesn't have a built-in connector to image data file format types like PNG or JPEG files like we're using here. To see the images, we're going to choose All Files, and then we'll select one of the images in the folder. We see the File.Contents function connects to our locally stored file to return a binary object. This stores our image data as a JPEG file, but we don't see any of the bitmap data, at least yet. To read the data in this binary object, let's insert another step below the source step. Let's read the data using the function binary to text on the source step to convert the binary file storing our image data into text that Power Query can read. We then need to concatenate another expression to the beginning of this formula. We'll use an ampersand to connect it. And we'll input data colon image forward slash jpeg. We're using JPEG before the base because the image is a JPEG file, but you can also configure this for other image format types like PNG. We'll also add a comma to the end of this expression. This creates a long bitmap text string that we can convert into an image. Now we created our image bitmap. Let's convert this value into a table without any delimiters. We'll set the column as Image and give it a text data type. We can then load our image data into our Power BI model to make sure they properly render in our visuals. As we load the data, let's select these image fields and set them as image URLs in the Data Category options. Finally, let's create table visuals with these new image fields to make sure they properly render and work. There we have it, image data imported into Power BI.

Setting up folder connection
